---
// Privacy policy for kinderform.org. Covers what the site does today and the features it may add.
// Before switching on any feature in "Things we may add", update the table there and the date below.
import Legal from "@/layouts/Legal.astro";

const h2 = "scroll-m-20 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight";
const h3 = "scroll-m-20 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight";
const p = "leading-7";
const ul = "ml-5 list-disc space-y-2 leading-7";
const link = "font-medium underline underline-offset-4";
const table = "w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm";
const th = "border-b px-2 py-2 align-top font-semibold";
const td = "border-b px-2 py-2 align-top leading-6";
const email = "jamie@kinderform.org";
---
<Legal
  title="Privacy policy: Kinderform"
  heading="Privacy policy"
  description="How Kinderform uses personal information, why, how long we keep it and your rights. The site sets no cookies and does not track you."
  current="/privacy"
  updated="28 November 2026"
>
  <div slot="summary" class="space-y-4">
    <p class="text-xl leading-8 text-muted-foreground">
      Kinderform collects as little about you as it can. Today the site sets no cookies and does not track you.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg border p-4">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold">The short version</h2>
      <ul class={`${ul} mt-2`}>
        <li>The example forms send nothing and store nothing. What you type stays in your browser.</li>
        <li>We only get your details if you email us, write on GitHub or join the WhatsApp community.</li>
        <li>We never sell your information, and we never use it to score, rank or screen anyone.</li>
        <li>Kinderform holds no information about children.</li>
        <li>You can ask to see, correct or delete what we hold. Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`} class={link}>{email}</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="who" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="who" class={h2}>Who is responsible for your information</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Kinderform is a free, non-commercial project run by Jamie Gannaway, a private individual who lives in Spain.
      It is not a company or a charity.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Jamie decides how your personal information is used. In data protection law, that makes him the
      "controller". In this policy, "we" and "us" mean Jamie.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Contact: <a href={`mailto:${email}`} class={link}>{email}</a>. Kinderform does not have a data protection
      officer, because the law does not require one for a project of this size.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      This policy follows the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and Spanish data protection law. For people
      in the UK, it also follows the UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="not" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="not" class={h2}>Kinderform is not a fostering or adoption service</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      We do not take enquiries about fostering or adopting, and we do not pass them on. The example forms are
      demonstrations. They send nothing to a service or to us.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      If you want to foster or adopt, contact a service directly. That service's own privacy notice covers what you
      send it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="now" class="space-y-6">
    <h2 id="now" class={h2}>What we collect today, and why</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      The law asks us to give a legal reason, called a "lawful basis", for each use. We give it for each item below.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>When you visit the site</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Our host, Vercel, keeps short technical records of each visit. They include your IP address, the page you asked
        for, the time and your browser type.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        We use these records only to keep the site working and safe, for example to stop attacks. We do not use them
        to find out who you are.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> our legitimate interest in running a safe, working website.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>Your light or dark mode choice</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        If you pick dark mode, your browser stores that choice on your device. We never see it. It is not a cookie, and
        it does not track you.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        The law lets sites store this kind of setting without asking first, because you asked for it. You can remove it
        by clearing your browser's site data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>The example forms and the form check</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        What you type into an example form stays in your browser. It is not sent to us or anyone else, and it is gone
        when you close the page.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Please type made-up details only. The forms are for trying out, not for real enquiries.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>When you email us</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        We get your email address, your name if you give it, and what you write. We use it to reply, to fix what you
        report and to improve Kinderform.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> our legitimate interest in answering you and improving the project.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        We will only quote you on the site or in research, even without your name, if you say we may.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>If you tell us about your own life</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Some people share their experience of fostering, adoption or care. Please leave out names, case details and
        anything that could identify a child.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Your message may include details the law treats as extra sensitive. Examples are your health, faith,
        sexuality or ethnic background.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        If it does, we will ask whether we may keep those details. If you agree, we keep them with your explicit
        consent. If you do not agree or do not answer within 30 days, we delete them.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> your explicit consent. You can withdraw it at any time by emailing us.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>When a service replies or asks for a correction</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Staff at fostering and adoption services may write to us about their service. We get their name, work email
        and what they write.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        We use this to correct our records and to give each service a right of reply. We never name individual staff
        on the site.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> our legitimate interest in publishing accurate, fair information.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>When you use GitHub</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Our code, data and research are on GitHub. Issues, comments and changes you post there are public. GitHub shows
        your username with them.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        GitHub runs its own service and is responsible for your GitHub account. We read and act on what you post.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> our legitimate interest in running an open project.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>When you join our WhatsApp community</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        As the community's admin, Jamie can see your phone number, your WhatsApp name and what you post. WhatsApp may
        also show your number to other members, depending on your settings.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        WhatsApp (Meta) runs its own service and is responsible for your WhatsApp account. You can leave the community
        at any time.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> our legitimate interest in hearing from people the project is for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>The facts we publish about services</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Our registers and tables describe services and their public web pages. A computer program reads those pages.
        A person checks what it finds, and that checking is not finished.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        We record the service, not its staff. If a public page names a person, we leave the name out. If you find one,
        tell us and we will remove it straight away.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="never" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="never" class={h2}>What we never do</h2>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li>We never sell, rent or trade your information.</li>
      <li>We never use it for advertising.</li>
      <li>We never score, rank, screen or predict people, and we make no automated decisions about you.</li>
      <li>We never collect information about children.</li>
      <li>We never add you to a mailing list unless you ask to join.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="share" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="share" class={h2}>Who else handles your information</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Jamie is the only person who reads your messages. These companies store or carry information for us:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={table}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>Company</th>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>What it does</th>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>Where</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Vercel Inc.</td>
            <td class={td}>Hosts the website and keeps its technical records</td>
            <td class={td}>United States and worldwide</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Our email provider</td>
            <td class={td}>Receives and stores email sent to {email}</td>
            <td class={td}>We will name it here and say where it stores email</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>GitHub, Inc.</td>
            <td class={td}>Hosts our public code, data and discussions</td>
            <td class={td}>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>WhatsApp (Meta)</td>
            <td class={td}>Runs the community chat</td>
            <td class={td}>European Union and worldwide</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class={p}>
      We may also share information if the law requires it, or to protect someone's safety.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="abroad" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="abroad" class={h2}>When your information leaves Europe</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Some of these companies store information in the United States or elsewhere outside the European Economic Area
      and the UK.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      When that happens, we rely on legal protections. One is an official finding that the country protects data well
      enough. The other is standard contract terms approved by the European Commission and the UK.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Email us for a copy of the protections that apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="keep" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="keep" class={h2}>How long we keep it</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class={table}>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>Information</th>
            <th scope="col" class={th}>How long</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Technical records of visits</td>
            <td class={td}>Vercel deletes them after a short time, usually days</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Emails</td>
            <td class={td}>2 years after our last message to each other, then we delete them</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Sensitive details you agreed we could keep</td>
            <td class={td}>Until you withdraw consent, and no longer than 2 years after our last contact</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>Corrections and replies from services</td>
            <td class={td}>While the related fact is on the site, and 2 years after that</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class={td}>GitHub posts and WhatsApp messages</td>
            <td class={td}>Until you delete them, or we close the project or community</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="later" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="later" class={h2}>Things we may add, and the promises that come with them</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      None of these are switched on today. We list them so you know our rules before we start.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Before we switch one on, we will update this page and name the company that runs it. Where the law asks for
      your consent, we will ask first.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>Visitor counts without cookies</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        We may count visits to each page with a tool that sets no cookies and does not follow you across sites. It
        would not store your full IP address.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> our legitimate interest in knowing which pages help people. You can object at
        any time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>Analytics that use cookies, such as Google Analytics</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        We would only load these if you say yes first. "No" will be as easy to choose as "Yes". The site works the same
        either way.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        You could change your mind at any time from a link at the bottom of every page. We would keep analytics data
        for no more than 14 months.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> your consent.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>A mailing list for updates</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        You would only join if you sign up and confirm by email. Every email would have a link to leave the list.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        We would keep your address until you leave, then delete it within 30 days.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> your consent.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>Accounts for services</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        Services might sign in to check or reply to their own entries. We would keep their name, work email and sign-in
        details.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        Accounts would be for adults at services only. We would delete an account within 30 days of a request to close
        it.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> to provide the account you asked for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h3 class={h3}>Forms that send us feedback or corrections</h3>
      <p class={p}>
        A form would send what you type to us, stored by a form company. Each form would say what it collects before
        you send it.
      </p>
      <p class={p}>
        <strong>Lawful basis and how long:</strong> the same as for emails, above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="rights" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="rights" class={h2}>Your rights</h2>
    <p class={p}>You have the right to:</p>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li>get a copy of the personal information we hold about you</li>
      <li>have it corrected if it is wrong</li>
      <li>have it deleted</li>
      <li>ask us to limit how we use it</li>
      <li>object to how we use it, when we rely on our legitimate interests</li>
      <li>get it in a format you can move to someone else</li>
      <li>withdraw your consent at any time, where we rely on it</li>
    </ul>
    <p class={p}>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`} class={link}>{email}</a>. We will reply within 1 month. We may ask you to
      confirm who you are. Using your rights is free.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Withdrawing consent does not make earlier use unlawful. Some rights have limits in law, and we will explain if
      one applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="complain" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="complain" class={h2}>If you are unhappy</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Please tell us first, so we can put it right. You can also complain to a data protection authority:
    </p>
    <ul class={ul}>
      <li>
        in Spain, the <a href="https://www.aepd.es" class={link}>Spanish Data Protection Agency (AEPD)</a>, which
        oversees this project
      </li>
      <li>
        in the UK, the <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" class={link}>Information Commissioner's Office</a>
      </li>
      <li>in another EU country, the authority where you live or work</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="safe" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="safe" class={h2}>How we keep it safe</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      The site uses an encrypted connection. Its security settings stop it loading code or content from other sites.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      Only Jamie can read messages sent to us. If something goes wrong that puts your information at risk, we will
      tell the authority within 72 hours where the law requires. We will tell you if the risk to you is high.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="children" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="children" class={h2}>Children</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      Kinderform is for adults. It is not meant for children, and we do not knowingly collect information from anyone
      under 18.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      If you think a child has sent us information, tell us and we will delete it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="links" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="links" class={h2}>Other websites</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      We link to services, research and other sites. They have their own privacy policies. We are not responsible for
      what they do with your information.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changes" class="space-y-4">
    <h2 id="changes" class={h2}>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p class={p}>
      We will update this page when anything changes, and change the date at the top. Every past version stays public
      in the project's history on GitHub.
    </p>
    <p class={p}>
      If a change means we would use your information in a new way, we will tell you before it starts, where we can.
    </p>
  </section>
</Legal>
